⚠️ 1. What is a Memory Leak?

A memory leak happens when objects are no longer needed but are still referenced in memory.

👉 Garbage Collector cannot remove them.

---

# 📌 Example of Memory Leak

```java id="m1"
import java.util.ArrayList;

class Demo {
    public static void main(String[] args) {

        ArrayList<Object> list = new ArrayList<>();

        while (true) {
            list.add(new Object());
        }
    }
}
```

---

🔍 Problem

✔ Objects keep getting stored
✔ References remain inside list
✔ Heap memory becomes full

👉 Causes:

```text id="m2"
OutOfMemoryError
```

---

🎯 Causes of Memory Leaks

* Unused object references
* Static collections
* Unclosed resources
* Event listeners not removed

---

✅ Preventing Memory Leaks

✔ Remove unnecessary references
✔ Close files and database connections
✔ Use weak references when needed
✔ Avoid excessive static objects

---

🔹 Example

```java id="m3"
obj = null;
```

👉 Makes object eligible for GC.

---

🧩 2. Reference Types in Java

Java provides different reference types for memory-sensitive applications.

---

📊 Types of References

1️⃣ Strong Reference
2️⃣ Weak Reference
3️⃣ Soft Reference
4️⃣ Phantom Reference

-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

🔹 1. Strong Reference

Default reference type.

```java id="r1"
Student s = new Student();
```

✔ Object will NOT be garbage collected while reference exists.

---

🔹 2. Weak Reference

Object can be garbage collected if only weak references exist.

---

📌 Example

```java id="r2"
import java.lang.ref.WeakReference;

Student s = new Student();

WeakReference<Student> weakRef =
        new WeakReference<>(s);
```

---

🎯 Uses

✔ Caching
✔ Avoiding memory leaks

---

🔹 3. Soft Reference

Object remains until JVM needs memory.

---

📌 Example

```java id="r3"
import java.lang.ref.SoftReference;

SoftReference<Student> softRef =
        new SoftReference<>(new Student());
```

---

🎯 Uses

✔ Memory-sensitive caches

---

🔹 4. Phantom Reference

Used for advanced memory management.

```java id="r4"
import java.lang.ref.PhantomReference;
```

👉 Cannot directly access object.

---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

📊 Comparison of Reference Types

| Reference Type | GC Behavior                  |
| -------------- | ---------------------------- |
| Strong         | Never collected immediately  |
| Weak           | Collected quickly            |
| Soft           | Collected when memory needed |
| Phantom        | Used for cleanup tracking    |

---

⚡ Real-World Uses

* Image caching
* Framework memory optimization
* JVM internals
* Resource cleanup systems

---

⚠️ Common Mistakes

❌ Keeping unnecessary object references
❌ Using static collections carelessly
❌ Forgetting to close resources

---

🧠 Real Insight

👉 Even with Garbage Collection, developers must manage references properly to avoid memory issues.

----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

📚 Key Takeaways

✔ Memory leaks happen due to unused references
✔ Garbage Collector cannot remove referenced objects
✔ Java provides multiple reference types
✔ Weak & Soft references help optimize memory usage

